Constructor-
	A constructor is a special member of a class that is automatically called when an object is created. Its primary purpose is to initialize the object's data (instance variables). A constructor has the same name as the class and does not have a return type, not even void.
	
Types of Constructors
	1. Default Constructor
		If you don't write any constructor, Java automatically provides a default constructor.
	2. No-Argument Constructor
		A constructor you write that takes no parameters.
	3. Parameterized Constructor
		Accepts values during object creation.

Constructor Overloading
	A class can have multiple constructors with different parameter lists.
	like - student(), student(String name), student(String name, int age)
	
Important Keyword: this (learn about it yourself <*\_/*> )

Again - A constructor is a special member of a class that is automatically invoked when an object is created. Its purpose is to initialize the object's instance variables. A constructor has the same name as the class and does not have a return type. Java supports default, no-argument, and parameterized constructors, and constructors can be overloaded but not overridden.

What is Constructor Chaining?
	Constructor chaining is the process of calling one constructor from another constructor to avoid code duplication and reuse initialization logic.
	Java provides two ways to do this:
		this() → Calls another constructor in the same class.
		super() → Calls the constructor of the parent class.
		
		```
			class Student {

			    String name;
			    int age;

			    Student() {
				this("Unknown", 18);
			    }

			    Student(String name) {
				this(name, 18);
			    }

			    Student(String name, int age) {
				this.name = name;
				this.age = age;
			    }
			}
		```
		Rules for this()
			Rule 1
				It must be the first statement inside a constructor.
			Rule 2
				A constructor can call only one constructor using this().
				
		super() -> we'll learn that in inheritance.
		Difference between this() and super()
		this()								super()
		Calls another constructor in the same class			Calls a constructor of the parent class
		Used for constructor chaining within a class			Used to initialize the parent part of an object
		Must be the first statement					Must also be the first statement
		Cannot be used together in the same constructor			Cannot be used together in the same constructor
		
