Destructor - 
	A destructor is a special method that is automatically called when an object is destroyed to release resources such as memory or file handles. Java does not have destructors. Instead, Java uses Garbage Collection (GC) to automatically reclaim memory occupied by objects that are no longer reachable.


In languages like C++, a destructor is a special function that runs automatically when an object is destroyed.

example - 
```
	class Student {
	public:
	    Student() {
		cout << "Constructor Called";
	    }

	    ~Student() {
		cout << "Destructor Called";
	    }
	};
```

Here:
	Constructor → Runs when the object is created.
	Destructor → Runs when the object is destroyed.
	
finalize() - 
	Older versions of Java had a method called finalize().
	However:
		finalize() is deprecated (since Java 9) and should not be used.
		There is no guarantee it will be called before an object is collected.
		It was removed because it caused performance and reliability issues.
		
Constructor vs Destructor
Constructor						Destructor
Creates/initializes an object				Cleans up when an object is destroyed
Java supports constructors				Java does not support destructors
Called automatically during object creation		In Java, memory cleanup is handled by the Garbage Collector

System.gc() request to the JVM to perform garbage collection. The JVM may ignore it.

Java does not have destructors. Instead, it uses an automatic Garbage Collector to reclaim memory occupied by objects that are no longer reachable. For non-memory resources such as files, sockets, or database connections, Java relies on explicit cleanup, preferably using try-with-resources.

