Instance variable vs Local variable

Instance Variable
	An instance variable is a variable declared inside a class but outside any method, constructor, or block. Each object of the class gets its own copy of the instance variables.

Local Variable
	A local variable is a variable declared inside a method, constructor, or block. It exists only during the execution of that method or block.
	
Memory
	Instance Variables
		Stored inside the object (on the Heap).
		Every object gets its own copy.
	Local Variables
		Stored in the Stack Memory.
Lifetime
	Instance Variable
		Lives as long as the object exists.
	Local Variable
		Exists only while the method is executing.
Default Values
	Instance Variables
		Java automatically initializes them.
	Local Variables
		Java does not initialize them.
		
Access
	Instance Variable
		Accessible by every instance method of the class.
	Local Variable
		Accessible only inside the method where it is declared.
		
		
An instance variable is declared inside a class but outside methods and belongs to an object. Each object has its own copy, and Java automatically initializes it with default values. A local variable is declared inside a method, constructor, or block, exists only during that method's execution, and must be initialized before use.

